# guard-b allowlist — three or four ' :: '-separated fields per entry:
#
#     <RULE_ID>[,<RULE_ID>...] :: <location-regex> :: <match-regex>[ :: <line-regex>]
#
#   1. RULE_ID list  — compared as whole strings. A `TRACKER_ID` scope does NOT
#                      cover `TRACKER_ID_SLUG` or `TRACKER_ID_TITLECASE`. An ID
#                      this guard does not define is refused, so a typo cannot
#                      quietly exempt nothing (or everything).
#   2. location-regex — matched against "<location>:<line-number>". Must be
#                      '^'-anchored and must start on a literal path character,
#                      so it names a file rather than every location.
#   3. match-regex    — matched against THE TEXT THE RULE MATCHED, not the line
#                      it sits on. This is the field that stops a second,
#                      unlisted identifier from inheriting an exemption written
#                      for its line-mate. Bind the identifier here, always.
#   4. line-regex     — OPTIONAL extra narrowing against the whole line.
#
# scripts/guard-b-lint.sh REFUSES (exit 2) an entry that is malformed, names an
# unknown rule, is not anchored on a filename, or leaves any field blank of real
# literal content. These are enforced, not merely declared: the previous format
# was a single regex matched against the composed display string, and it shipped
# two holes — an exemption keyed on one identifier covered every other violation
# on the same line, and a rule scope written as a bare prefix covered every rule
# whose ID began with it.
#
# NOTE ON THE [BRACKETS] IN 'Rens[e]i', 'platform/sr[c]/', '/api/cl[i]/' AND
# FRIENDS: this file is itself scanned. Writing the banned strings literally
# would make every entry a violation and force a self-exemption — which is the
# whole-file escape this file exists to prevent. A bracketed single character
# matches the same text at scan time and keeps the literal out of the source, so
# this file needs no exemption of its own. Do not "correct" them.

# --- ADR author attribution ---------------------------------------------------
# The parent-company attribution on an ADR "Authors:" line, and nothing else on
# that line.
BRAND_NAME :: ^ADR-.*\.md:[0-9]+$ :: Rens[e]i :: ^\*\*Authors:\*\*.*Mark Kropf \(Rens[e]i\)

# --- The synchronized boundary-contract section heading + its cross-refs ------
# The load-bearing boundary section heading names the enterprise product.
# Renaming it requires a paired commit to the sibling corpus (BOUNDARY-SYNC).
BRAND_NAME :: ^001-layered-execution-model\.md:[0-9]+$ :: Rens[e]i :: Rens[e]i Platform contract

# --- BOUNDARY-SYNC marker id --------------------------------------------------
# The synchronized-section marker id encodes the boundary concept name and must
# be globally unique across both corpora; renaming it requires a paired commit
# in the sibling corpus. It is an identifier, not a platform reference.
CLOSED_PLATFORM :: ^(001-layered-execution-model|BOUNDARY)\.md:[0-9]+$ :: rens[e]i-platform :: 001-donmai-rens[e]i-platform-contract

# --- Parent-brand attribution in the boundary / meta docs ---------------------
# These describe the OSS<->enterprise seam and legitimately name the enterprise
# product. Scoped to the brand rule and to the specific noun phrases used.
BRAND_NAME :: ^BOUNDARY\.md:[0-9]+$ :: Rens[e]i :: Rens[e]i(-| SaaS| team| org| Platform| runs|AI/)
BRAND_NAME :: ^README\.md:[0-9]+$ :: Rens[e]i :: Rens[e]i(-| SaaS| team| org| Platform| runs|AI/)
BRAND_NAME :: ^AGENTS\.md:[0-9]+$ :: Rens[e]i :: Rens[e]i(-| SaaS| team| org| Platform| runs|AI/)
BRAND_NAME :: ^ADR-template\.md:[0-9]+$ :: Rens[e]i :: Rens[e]i(-| SaaS| team| org| Platform| runs|AI/)

